// END AUTHORSHIP ADDON
// =====================================================
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - DVSM v16.x README / SYSTEM SPECIFICATION / WP / BP
// =====================================================
//
// TITLE: DVSM v16.x — Identifiability-Gated, Regime-Aware,
//        Cryptographically Auditable Execution Substrate
//
// STATUS: ARCHITECTURAL CONSOLIDATION (RESEARCH → IMPLEMENTABLE CORE)
//
// GOAL:
// A deterministic execution system that remains:
//   - Identifiable under noise
//   - Stable under regime shifts
//   - Auditable under adversarial conditions
//   - Replayable across heterogeneous environments
//   - Latency-aware at “gaming-class responsiveness” scale
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 0. SYSTEM OVERVIEW
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM v16.x is a layered computational substrate combining:
//
//   1. Deterministic Execution Core
//   2. Bayesian + Spectral Identifiability Layer
//   3. Hierarchical Noise Geometry Model
//   4. Physical Execution Verification (BIH + DAI)
//   5. Regime Change Detection (GCPF / MDD)
//   6. Temporal Drift Control (TIE / DRA)
//   7. Cryptographic Audit DAG (Merkle lineage)
//
// The system treats computation as a physically-anchored,
// statistically-verifiable event stream.
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 1. CORE DESIGN PRINCIPLES
// =====================================================
//
// 🧮 Determinism First
// - All state transitions are replayable
// - No hidden mutation paths
// - Execution is envelope-based
//
// 🧠 Identifiability Over Accuracy
// - A model is invalid if H0/H1 are indistinguishable
// - Loss of separability triggers halting conditions
//
// 🛡 Physical Trust Boundary (BIH)
// - Hardware response curves define identity
// - Drift in latency fingerprints = observer mutation
//
// 🌐 Regime Awareness
// - System behavior is segmented into regimes
// - Each regime has its own statistical validity domain
//
// ⏱ Temporal Coherence
// - Drift is allowed only if second-order bounded
// - “Smooth evolution” is enforced geometrically
//
// 🔐 Cryptographic Auditability
// - Every state is hashed into a Merkle DAG
// - Global root defines system-wide truth snapshot
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 2. LATENCY-FIRST DESIGN (GAMING-CLASS TARGET)
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM explicitly supports low-latency execution goals:
//
// ⚡ Sub-millisecond decision paths (hot path)
// ⚡ Precomputed regime classification (no runtime inference stalls)
// ⚡ Shard-local covariance caching
// ⚡ Incremental Merkle updates (O(1) per ingestion)
//
// Latency invariants:
//
//   - No global recomputation on pulse()
//   - No blocking Bayesian inference in execution plane
//   - All heavy math moved to regime pre-pass
//
// Result:
//   → Predictable execution under high-frequency workloads
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 3. SECURITY MODEL (STATE-LEVEL OPTIONAL HARDENING)
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM security is layered and optional per deployment:
//
// 🛡 Level 1: Statistical Security
//   - Identifiability gating (MDD)
//   - Posterior overlap thresholds
//
// 🛡 Level 2: Geometric Security
//   - Noise tensor consistency (Wasserstein bounds)
//   - Cross-shard manifold coherence
//
// 🛡 Level 3: Physical Security
//   - BIH fingerprinting (CPU/cache response curves)
//   - Execution differential probing (DAI)
//
// 🛡 Level 4: Cryptographic Security
//   - Merkle DAG state history
//   - SHA256-linked audit chain
//   - Actor-bound shard signatures
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 4. KEY PIONEERING MECHANISMS
// =====================================================
//
// 🧬 (A) Noise Geometry Tensor System
// ------------------------------------
// Each shard maintains:
//
//   - Local covariance tensor (Σ_i)
//   - Drift vector (μ_i)
//   - Global hyperprior alignment constraint
//
// Purpose:
//   Detect adversarial “silent steering” of local statistics
//
// ----------------------------------------------------
//
// 🧠 (B) Identifiability-Gated Execution
// --------------------------------------
// Execution is blocked if:
//
//   Posterior(H0) ≈ Posterior(H1)
//
// This enforces:
//   → “If you cannot distinguish states, you cannot act.”
//
// ----------------------------------------------------
//
// 🧪 (C) Differential Adversarial Interleaving (DAI)
// --------------------------------------------------
// Execution is paired:
//
//   control path (f_c)
//   probe path   (f_p)
//
// System checks:
//
//   Δ_observed ≈ Δ_predicted
//
// If not:
//   → instrumentation spoofing assumed
//
// ----------------------------------------------------
//
// 🧷 (D) Boundary Invariance Hashing (BIH)
// ----------------------------------------
// The system stores:
//
//   - CPU latency curves under stress
//   - Hardware identity tokens
//
// Any drift in response curve = identity change
//
// ----------------------------------------------------
//
// ⏳ (E) Time-Indexed Equivalence (TIE)
// ------------------------------------
// Instead of static equivalence:
//
//   T(C_i → C_j)
//
// System enforces:
//
//   d²T/dt² bounded
//
// Meaning:
//   → drift is allowed only if smooth over time
//
// ----------------------------------------------------
//
// 🌪 (F) Geometric Change-Point Filtering (GCPF)
// ---------------------------------------------
// Detects:
//
//   - non-differentiable jumps
//   - regime boundary collapse
//
// Triggers:
//
//   → system freeze / re-baselining
//
// ----------------------------------------------------
//
// 🔗 (G) Merkle DAG Execution History
// -----------------------------------
// Every ingestion becomes:
//
//   hash(parent + data + shard)
//
// Ensures:
//
//   → full replay determinism
//   → tamper-evident computation lineage
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 5. SYSTEM BEHAVIOR SUMMARY
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM v16.x behaves as:
//
//   A REPLAYABLE PHYSICAL-STATISTICAL MACHINE
//
// It does NOT assume:
//
//   ❌ stable environment
//   ❌ trusted observers
//   ❌ fixed hardware identity
//
// It DOES assume:
//
//   ✅ noise is structured
//   ✅ identity is measurable
//   ✅ drift is detectable
//   ✅ truth is regime-bounded
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 6. OPERATIONAL OUTCOME
// =====================================================
//
// The system produces:
//
//   - SSP (Stable State Proofs)
//   - Collapse Certificates (RCCs)
//   - Regime Transition Logs
//   - Full replay audit DAGs
//
// Failure modes are not hidden:
//
//   → they are classified, timestamped, and exported
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 7. FINAL DESIGN PHILOSOPHY
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM v16.x is not a model.
//
// It is:
//
//   "A constraint system for maintaining distinguishability
//    under adversarial statistical, physical, and temporal drift."
//
// Truth = separability under noise.
// Security = invariance under substitution.
// Execution = permissioned identifiability.
//
// =====================================================
// END OF README SPECIFICATION
// =====================================================
// =====================================================
// MARK: - DVSM v16.x WHITEPAPER ADDENDUM
// =====================================================
//
// TITLE: Formal Specification, Threat Model, and Evaluation Criteria
// ROLE: Bridges architectural system → research-grade whitepaper
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 1. PROBLEM STATEMENT
// =====================================================
//
// Traditional distributed systems fail under:
//
//   - Silent statistical drift
//   - Observer manipulation (instrumentation spoofing)
//   - Cross-node correlation attacks
//   - Hidden regime shifts in environment dynamics
//   - Loss of identifiability under high entropy
//
// Core failure mode:
//
//   Systems assume consistency of observation.
//
// DVSM rejects this assumption.
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 2. SYSTEM OBJECTIVE
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM is designed to maintain:
//
//   (A) Identifiability under uncertainty
//   (B) Execution correctness under adversarial noise
//   (C) Observable invariance under hardware drift
//   (D) Replayability under full system reconstruction
//
// Formal objective:
//
//   Preserve distinguishability of system states:
//
//       H0 ≠ H1  under all valid observation channels
//
// even when:
//
//   - observers are compromised
//   - hardware changes mid-execution
//   - statistical distributions shift
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 3. THREAT MODEL
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM assumes a strong adversary capable of:
//
//   🧠 Statistical attacks
//      - distribution shaping
//      - posterior collapse manipulation
//
//   🧬 Physical attacks
//      - microcode modification
//      - hypervisor-level spoofing
//
//   🌐 Network-level attacks
//      - shard synchronization poisoning
//      - cross-node correlation steering
//
//   ⏱ Temporal attacks
//      - delayed execution replay
//      - regime boundary exploitation
//
// Key constraint:
//
//   Adversary cannot violate physical entropy bounds,
//   but can shape its interpretation.
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 4. CORE ASSUMPTIONS
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM assumes:
//
//   1. Noise exists and is measurable
//   2. Hardware has observable response curves
//   3. Statistical drift is inevitable
//   4. Identity is relational, not absolute
//   5. Computation is replayable from traces
//
// DVSM does NOT assume:
//
//   ❌ trusted hardware
//   ❌ static distributions
//   ❌ stable observers
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 5. CORRECTNESS CRITERIA
// =====================================================
//
// A system state is valid iff:
//
//   (1) Identifiability holds:
//       posterior overlap < MDD threshold
//
//   (2) Geometric coherence holds:
//       Wasserstein(local, global) ≤ ε
//
//   (3) Physical invariance holds:
//       BIH fingerprint unchanged within tolerance
//
//   (4) Temporal stability holds:
//       second derivative of regime drift bounded
//
//   (5) Execution consistency holds:
//       Δ_control ≈ Δ_probe (DAI invariant)
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 6. FAILURE SEMANTICS
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM does NOT hide failure.
//
// It explicitly classifies:
//
//   🟥 IdentifiabilityLoss
//      → states cannot be distinguished
//
//   🟥 ObserverDrift
//      → hardware identity no longer stable
//
//   🟥 RegimeDiscontinuity
//      → non-continuous environmental shift
//
//   🟥 NoiseConsistencyFailure
//      → global/local statistical mismatch
//
//   🟥 ExecutionFailure
//      → instrumentation or probe mismatch
//
// Failure ALWAYS results in:
//
//   - halted execution OR
//   - arbitration escalation
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 7. EVALUATION METHODOLOGY
// =====================================================
//
// DVSM correctness is evaluated by:
//
//   (A) Replay determinism tests
//   (B) Adversarial perturbation injection
//   (C) Cross-shard consistency stress tests
//   (D) Hardware fingerprint stability trials
//   (E) Regime shift simulation benchmarks
//
// Success metric:
//
//   System remains functionally correct under:
//
//       increasing entropy + decreasing trust
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - 8. SECURITY POSTURE SUMMARY
// =====================================================
//
// Security in DVSM is not encryption-centric.
//
// It is:
//
//   "Detectability under transformation"
//
// If a system can be silently changed without detection,
// it is considered insecure.
//
// =====================================================
// MARK: - END ADDENDUM
// =====================================================
